channel-settings-mask-unknown-type = This network doesn’t advertise this { $prefix } mask type; it’s sent as typed and the server decides.
channel-settings-mask-no-extended-type = This network doesn’t offer { $prefix }… extended masks, so it reads this as a nickname.
channel-settings-mask-matches-none = No people match here
channel-settings-mask-matches-one = Matches 1 person here now: { $people }
channel-settings-mask-matches-many = Matches { $count } people here now: { $people }
channel-settings-mask-you = { $nick } (you)
channel-settings-mask-how = How masks work, with examples
channel-settings-mask-three-parts = A mask has three parts, nick!user@host:
channel-settings-mask-nick-about = Nick: the name people see. Anyone can change nick at any time, so a nick mask follows whoever uses the nick, not a person or their account.
channel-settings-mask-user-about = User: the username (ident) their client sends. A leading ~ means the server couldn’t verify it.
channel-settings-mask-host-about = Host: where they connect from, as the network shows it to everyone: an address, a hostname, or a cloak that hides it. /whois shows someone’s.
channel-settings-mask-wildcards = * matches any run of characters (even none) and ? exactly one; case doesn’t matter.
channel-settings-mask-accounts-supported = { $network } also takes account masks: { $mask } matches whoever is logged in to that services account. Masks without it never identify an account.
channel-settings-mask-accounts-unsupported = { $network } doesn’t advertise account masks, so masks here only match nick, user and host, and never identify an account.
channel-settings-mask-example-nick = Shorthand for alice!*@*: whoever uses the nick alice, now or later. Changing nick gets around it.
channel-settings-mask-example-address = Anyone connecting from the address 203.0.113.7.
channel-settings-mask-example-host = Anyone whose host ends in .example.net; on an internet provider’s hosts that can be a lot of people.
channel-settings-mask-example-username = Any username ending in alice: matches ~alice and alice, but also malice, from any host and with any nick.
channel-settings-mask-example-account = Whoever is logged in to the account alice, whatever nick or host they use.
channel-settings-mask-example-mute = Mutes alice!*@* instead of banning: they can stay but can’t talk.
channel-settings-mask-use-example = Use the example { $example }
channel-settings-mask-example-only = Choosing an example only fills in the field; nothing changes until you press Add.

